    "q-134281": {
      "dom_id": "question-134281",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Of the $2$ pounds of chocolate almonds that John bought, he gave his cousin Angela $\\frac{5}{6}$ pounds. As a mixed number, John had\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$pounds of chocolate almonds remaining.",
      "worked_solution": "To find out how many chocolate almonds John had left, we must calculate the difference $2-\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{5}{6}$ from $2,$ we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $6.$\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $6$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 6}{1\\times 6}=\\frac{12}{6}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{12-5}{6}=\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n7÷6=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, John has $1\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ pounds of chocolate almonds left.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
